12. What is different between Spring Boot Application and Spring MVC?
Answer: The core difference between Spring Boot and Spring MVC lies in their purpose and scope within the Spring ecosystem.
Spring MVC:
It is a web framework built on the Spring Framework, specifically designed to facilitate the development of web applications following the Model-View-Controller (MVC) architectural pattern. It provides the necessary 
components and infrastructure for request handling, routing, view rendering, and data binding in a web environment.
Spring Boot:
It is a framework that simplifies the setup and configuration of Spring-based applications, including those using Spring MVC. It takes an opinionated approach, providing sensible defaults 
and auto-configuration capabilities to reduce boilerplate code and accelerate the development process. Spring Boot can be used to create standalone, production-ready applications with minimal configuration.

